                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_strategic:     `<strong>Right call — this is a relationship to transform, not a vendor to punish.</strong> It's your single largest indirect spend, you're operationally dependent on Apex, and you need their innovation. Penalizing harder under the same contract just deepens the hole. Now name <em>why</em> it plateaued.`,
                    coach_r1_transactional: `<strong>Treating $64M of strategic dependency as a rate-card exercise is the squeeze reflex.</strong> Sharpen the rate card and tighten the SLA, and you reward Apex for billing more activity — the very thing that's failing you. The design is the problem, not the price.`,
                    coach_r1_rebid:         `<strong>Pulling the re-bid trigger on day one throws away nine years and 9–12 months of transition risk.</strong> Switching costs $6–9M with real service risk on your e-commerce accounts. Re-bid is a BATNA, not an opening move.`,
                    coach_r1_performance:   `<strong>“Apex is just underperforming” is exactly the wrong diagnosis.</strong> Penalize harder and you fight the symptom. Apex isn't lazy — it's rational: every efficiency you ask for cuts its own revenue. Fix the incentive, not the scorecard.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a strategic relationship AND found the structural driver — the inverted incentive that makes Apex's profit move against your savings. That's the lens that makes a Vested redesign possible instead of another fee fight.`,
                    coach_r1_lowconviction: `<strong>A tentative diagnosis won't survive the negotiation.</strong> If you don't commit to the contract-design frame, you'll drift back to haggling rates the moment the CFO pushes — and you'll be back here next peak season.`,
                    coach_r2_squeeze:       `<strong>You've negotiated Apex's rates down to hit the CFO's number.</strong> Congratulations — for one year. Now Apex bleeds margin on your account and has even <em>less</em> reason to fund the automation that would take real cost out. What happens to service next peak, and to the $7.7M in year two?`,
                    coach_r2_performance:   `<strong>Performance-based is a real step up — but it still pays per activity.</strong> SLAs with bonuses sharpen behavior, yet Apex still earns more when you ship more activity. You've improved the scorecard without curing the inverted incentive. Half a fix.`,
                    coach_r2_vested:        `<strong>Outcome-based is the model that fits this dependency.</strong> Buying cost-per-order and perfect-order-rate instead of picks and pallets unlocks the automation Apex was stalling — because now efficiency is what they're paid for. Make sure the economics in the next round actually reward it.`,
                    coach_r2_howtrap:       `<strong>You picked Vested, then specified the “how”.</strong> Dictating pick method, slotting and staffing ratios out-engineers your own logistics expert and locks out their innovation. Vested defines the <em>outcome</em> and lets Apex find the method. Are you buying their compliance or their ingenuity?`,
                    coach_r2_overcut:       `<strong>Even inside a partnership frame, a double-digit rate cut reignites the fight.</strong> You can't ask Apex to invest in your outcomes while squeezing their margin to the bone. The saving has to come from the unlocked efficiency, not from bleeding the supplier.`,
                    coach_r3_noshare:       `<strong>You called it Vested and added a charter — but Apex still gets paid per pick.</strong> With essentially no gainshare, your automation still cuts their revenue $4.8M. You renamed the relationship without changing a single incentive. Where exactly does Apex earn <em>more</em> by helping you spend <em>less</em>?`,
                    coach_r3_aligned:       `<strong>Now the economics move together.</strong> A gainshare in the textbook range means Apex profits from the savings it helps create — so it funds the automation instead of stalling it. The inverted incentive is finally inverted back. Protect it with the baseline.`,
                    coach_r3_toogenerous:   `<strong>You've given away too much of the CFO's saving.</strong> A gainshare this rich aligns Apex beautifully but leaves little net for CHB — and the CFO's $7.7M is what's on the line. Align the incentive without surrendering the mandate.`,
                    coach_r3_nobaseline:    `<strong>You've promised a gainshare against… what baseline?</strong> Apex will argue the savings came from your volume; you'll argue it was the rate. With no agreed baseline and no open-book, your win-win is next year's dispute. Value has to be <em>measured</em> before it's split.`,
                    coach_r3_penalty:       `<strong>Keeping the penalty-heavy SLA sends every surprise to legal.</strong> A new channel, a tariff, a demand collapse — none are in your document. A relational contract sends those to a joint governance body with guiding principles. How will you handle the contingency you didn't write down?`,
                    coach_r4_winwin:        `<strong>That's the case that lands with Apex.</strong> Showing in dollars that funding the automation earns them more than stalling it — growth and gainshare replacing the lost billable picks — turns your counterpart into a co-investor. This is what makes the renegotiation credible rather than coercive.`,
                    coach_r4_mandate:       `<strong>“Absorb the 12% or we re-bid” is the squeeze in a partnership costume.</strong> You've told Apex the upside is all yours and the pain is all theirs. That's not a Vested deal — it's the old adversarial contract with new vocabulary, and Apex will treat it that way.`,
                    coach_r4_charter:       `<strong>Leading with the partnership language and a charter, with the economics unproven, is Vested-in-name-only.</strong> Apex has heard “partner” before. Trust follows aligned dollars, not the other way round — show the money first.`,
                    coach_r4_headline:      `<strong>Promising a clean 12% and netting it out later is how the savings unravel in year two.</strong> The CFO will hold you to the headline; when the gainshare and the real TCO net it down, you own the gap. Prove the number on TCO now, not after signing.`,
                    coach_r4_soft:          `<strong>Asking the CFO to relax the mandate cedes the hardest argument.</strong> The relationship <em>is</em> worth more than the number — but the way to prove that is the TCO case, not a plea. Show that the aligned deal beats both the squeeze and the re-bid on real cost.`,
                    coach_r4_bluff:         `<strong>You're using a re-bid you can't afford as a bluff, and Apex can see it.</strong> Transition is 9–12 months and $6–9M with service risk on your e-commerce accounts — Apex knows you know that. Bluff it and they call it; the leverage you had as an honest floor just evaporated.`,
                    coach_r4_landed:        `<strong>That's the deal, defended on both sides.</strong> Apex sees where it earns more by helping you save; the CFO sees the $7.7M proven on TCO, net of the gainshare and against a re-bid that's slower, costlier and riskier. The aligned, outcome-based deal beats the squeeze and the bluff — that's the renegotiation done right.`,

